# sha256 of normalised third-party names this repository must never contain.
#
# Plaintext, this file would BE the leak — a list of the real companies and
# people a demo pipeline was run against, published permanently in git history.
# Hashed, it still fails the build on an exact match and reveals nothing.
#
# Add an entry only AFTER the name is out of the tree, or the check is
# permanently red and somebody deletes it:
#
#     .github/scripts/check-third-party-names.py --add "Some Company"
#
# Matching is on lowercase-alphanumeric shingles of 1-3 pieces, so casing,
# underscores, hyphens and camelCase are all irrelevant: BioRenew, biorenew,
# BIORENEW_A and bio-renew are one entry.
#
# Names that are ordinary English words or well-known technology products are
# deliberately EXCLUDED: this repository legitimately discusses vector
# databases and eval tooling by name, and a guard that fires on those is a
# guard somebody turns off.
#
# Entries shorter than about five characters are EXCLUDED: matching is on
# concatenated shingles, so a four-letter name reassembles out of ordinary
# text. `ssip` matched `"blocks %s (%s)", (ip` in an IP-address test. A guard
# that fails on a line containing no name at all is one nobody believes.
